\chapter{Caching}
\label{cache-module}

Academic database APIs present a fundamental tension: we need fresh results to
discover new research, but we also need stable results for reproducible
reviews.
Worse, most APIs impose rate limits that make repeated queries impractical
during iterative workflow refinement.

This chapter implements a caching layer that sits between providers and the
network, transparently storing and retrieving search results.
The cache is invisible during normal operation---searches just work faster---but
users can inspect and clear it when they need fresh data.

Caching provides two distinct benefits:
\begin{description}
\item[Rate limit management] Semantic Scholar allows roughly one request per
  second without an API key; Web of Science is even stricter.
  Caching lets users refine queries, regenerate reports, and resume sessions
  without hitting these limits.
\item[Reproducibility] A systematic review must document exactly which papers
  were found.
  Cached results ensure that re-running a search weeks later produces identical
  output, supporting methodological rigor.
\end{description}

We deliberately avoid cache expiration.
Fresh results require explicit action ([[scholar cache clear]]), because
implicit staleness would undermine reproducibility---users would not know
whether differences stem from new publications or cache invalidation.


\section{Design overview}

We implement caching using the [[cachetools]] library with the [[@cached]]
decorator.
Each provider maintains its own cache dictionary, which we persist to disk
using [[pickle]].
The cache never expires automatically---users clear it manually when they want
fresh results.
Several [[scholar]] processes often share one cache directory, so a save
adds to the file under a lock instead of replacing it, writes
atomically, never overwrites a file it cannot read, and is skipped for a
cache the process did not change (\cref{sec:cache-concurrency}).

The cache files are stored in a platform-appropriate location:
\begin{itemize}
\item Linux: [[~/.cache/scholar/]]
\item macOS: [[~/Library/Caches/scholar/]]
\item Windows: [[C:\Users\<user>\AppData\Local\scholar\Cache\]]
\end{itemize}

