\section{Loading and saving caches}
\label{sec:cache-load-save}

Each provider has its own cache file, named after the provider.
We use Python's native binary serialization because it handles the [[Paper]]
dataclass objects without any additional configuration.

\subsection{Why Not JSON?}

We considered JSON for human-readable cache files, but chose binary
serialization because:
\begin{description}
\item[Dataclass support] Binary format handles [[Paper]] objects natively.
  JSON would require custom encoder/decoder classes.
\item[Type preservation] Binary format preserves Python types (datetime, etc.)
  exactly. JSON would require type annotations or conventions.
\item[Simplicity] No need to maintain JSON schemas as data structures evolve.
\end{description}

The tradeoff is that cache files aren't human-readable, but since they're
only for performance optimization (not user data), this is acceptable.
User-generated content (notes) uses JSON for the opposite reasons.

\subsection{Several processes, one cache directory}
\label{sec:cache-concurrency}

Users and agents routinely run several [[scholar]] processes at once: a
long [[scholar enrich]] in one terminal, searches in another, an agent's
checks in the background.
Each process loads the caches it needs when their modules are imported
and writes them back when it exits (\cref{sec:cache-autosave}).
The first version of this module wrote each file in place, with a plain
[[open(..., "wb")]], and treated a file it could not read as an empty
cache.
Together the two emptied the real cache directory
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/175}).
One process was half-way through rewriting [[s2.pkl]] when another read
it.
The reader got a truncated pickle, took it for an empty cache, and at
exit wrote that empty cache over the writer's complete file.
Every entry in a search cache stands for a request that cost quota, and
the synthesis caches hold paid model output, so the loss could not be
repaired by anything but paying again.

Four rules now govern how a cache reaches its file.
\begin{description}
\item[A file that cannot be read is never written over]
  [[load_cache]] still returns an empty cache, so the program works, but
  the save does not trust that emptiness: it reads the file again, under
  the lock, before writing.
  If the file reads now (the writer has finished), the save merges with
  it as usual.
  If it still does not, the file is left exactly as it is and this
  process's entries go to a file beside it,
  [[<name>.pkl.unmerged-<pid>]], with a warning naming both.
  The cache is then not lost but no longer used either, so the warning
  is what tells the user to look.
\item[A save adds to the file instead of replacing it]
  Under an exclusive lock ([[utils.file_lock]] on a hidden sidecar
  [[.<name>.pkl.lock]], \cref{sec:file-lock}), the save re-reads the
  file and writes the union of its entries and ours.
  On a key that both hold, our entry wins.
  The key names the request (the query, the limit, the filters, a cache
  version), so both values answer the same request and either may be
  served; taking ours keeps the rule simple and keeps the file in step
  with what this process showed its user.
  Since the in-memory cache started as a copy of the file, the union
  also repairs a file that a process without the lock---an older
  [[scholar]] still installed somewhere---emptied or cut short.
  The lock is what makes the merge sound: without it, two processes
  could both re-read the old file and the second to write would drop
  the first one's entries.
\item[Writes are atomic] The merged cache goes to a temporary file in
  the same directory, which is flushed to disk and renamed over the old
  one ([[utils.atomic_write_bytes]], \cref{sec:atomic-write}).
  A reader therefore sees the old file or the new one, never a part of
  either; that removes the truncated reads that started the loss.
\item[An unchanged cache is not saved] Most processes only read their
  caches: [[sessions show]], a search that the cache answers, an import
  check.
  With merging, saving such a cache would cost a full re-read and
  re-serialisation of a file that can be tens of megabytes, at every
  exit, for nothing.
  Skipping it keeps the cost of an exit proportional to what the process
  changed, and it gives the strongest guarantee of all to the commonest
  case: a process that changed nothing cannot change any file.
  We measured the exit hook with twenty caches of 12.7\,MB in total
  (November 2026, one laptop, best of three): nothing changed took
  under a millisecond, one changed cache 68\,ms, all twenty 2.1\,s.
  Writing all twenty in place, as every exit used to, took 0.5\,s:
  merging costs a re-read and two [[fsync]]s per changed cache, which is
  why the skip matters.
\end{description}

What this does not cover: an older [[scholar]] without these rules may
still run alongside, writing in place and without the lock.
Our atomic writes cannot give it a partial file, but its writes can give
us one, which the first rule then protects instead of overwriting.
And the lock coordinates processes on one machine only, as for sessions
(\cref{sec:file-lock}).

Nor is the lock unconditional.
[[file_lock]] gives up after a timeout and lets the body run unlocked,
so that one stuck process cannot hang every other
(\cref{sec:file-lock}).
An unlocked save is still atomic, so it never leaves a truncated file,
but it can lose entries: if another process replaces the file between
our re-read and our replace, our replace drops what that process added.
The default timeout of ten seconds was chosen for sessions, whose saves
hold the lock for milliseconds.
A cache save holds it for a re-read and a write of the whole file, which
grows with the cache: we measured 0.6\,s for a changed 6\,MB cache
(November 2026, one laptop).
With many processes exiting at once on one large cache, the last in the
queue would wait for all the others, and ten seconds runs out after a
handful.
Cache saves therefore wait up to [[CACHE_LOCK_TIMEOUT]], a minute.
Waiting costs little: only an exiting process waits, and it waits long
only behind a queue or a holder that is stuck (suspended mid-save, say);
not waiting costs entries that were paid for.
Past the minute the save goes ahead unlocked with [[file_lock]]'s
warning, and the loss described above becomes possible.
A session save and a notes write refuse instead of writing unlocked
(\cref{sec:session-lock} in the review chapter, \cref{sec:notes-lock}
in the notes chapter), and a cache save deliberately does not
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/319}).  What a session
or a note holds is the user's own work, which nothing can recreate, so
the lost update is worse than a refusal.  A cache holds answers that
can be fetched again: the unlocked save is atomic and merges with the
file, so it never corrupts it and loses at most the entries another
process added in that moment, which a later search fetches again at
the price of requests.  A refusal would lose this process's entries
outright instead, since a cache is saved at exit, when nobody is left
to be told or to retry; it would trade a possible loss for a certain
one.
[[cache clear]] keeps the default: it is interactive, and an unlocked
delete loses no entry; at worst a save under way undoes it.

<<constants>>=
CACHE_LOCK_TIMEOUT = 60  # seconds; a cache save re-reads and writes it all
@

The rest of the section builds the pieces in the order a cache meets
them: the dictionary that knows whether it changed, the loading, and the
saving.

\subsection{Knowing whether a cache changed}

To skip unchanged caches the save must know whether a cache changed.
Comparing key sets at exit would miss a value replaced under an existing
key, and keeping a copy of every cache to compare against would double
its memory.
Instead [[load_cache]] returns a [[dict]] subclass that sets a flag when
it is changed.
The providers keep using it as the plain dictionary they always had
(they read and assign items, and [[cachetools]]' decorators do the
same).

Every mutating method has to set the flag, not only [[__setitem__]]:
the C implementations of [[dict.update]], [[setdefault]], [[pop]] and
[[clear]] do not go through [[__setitem__]], so overriding that alone
would let an [[update]] slip by unsaved.
What the flag cannot see is a change \emph{inside} a value: after
[[cache[key]["field"] = x]] the cache holds the same object under the
same key, and nothing tells it the object changed.
Code that adds to a cached value must therefore assign the key anew,
[[cache[key] = {**cache[key], "field": x}]], as the synthesis does for
its report abstract (\cref{llm-review-module}).
The provider caches only ever assign whole values.
The file always receives a plain [[dict]] ([[save_cache]] copies the
entries), so cache files stay readable by older versions of
[[scholar]], which matters while one is still installed.

<<cache load and save functions>>=
class PersistentCache(dict):
    """A cache dictionary that remembers whether it was changed.

    `load_cache` returns one, and `save_cache` skips it while `changed`
    is false.  Saving it successfully clears the flag.
    """

    changed = False

    def __setitem__(self, key, value):
        super().__setitem__(key, value)
        self.changed = True

    def __delitem__(self, key):
        super().__delitem__(key)
        self.changed = True

    def __ior__(self, other):
        self.update(other)
        return self

    def clear(self):
        super().clear()
        self.changed = True

    def pop(self, key, *default):
        if key in self:
            self.changed = True
        return super().pop(key, *default)

    def popitem(self):
        item = super().popitem()
        self.changed = True
        return item

    def setdefault(self, key, default=None):
        if key not in self:
            self.changed = True
        return super().setdefault(key, default)

    def update(self, *args, **kwargs):
        super().update(*args, **kwargs)
        self.changed = True
@

\subsection{Loading a cache}

Loading and saving both read the file, so the reading is one function.
It tells the three outcomes apart, because they call for different
actions: no file is an empty cache, a readable file is its entries, and
an unreadable file is an empty cache \emph{and} an error that the save
must respect.
We catch every exception the unpickling raises, not only the
[[pickle]] and I/O errors.
A damaged file can make [[pickle]] raise almost anything
([[EOFError]] for a truncated one, [[AttributeError]] or
[[ModuleNotFoundError]] when a pickled class has moved, [[ValueError]],
[[IndexError]]), and before, anything outside the short list crashed the
import of the provider module.
Catching broadly is safe now because a file we could not read is kept,
whatever the reason.
A file that unpickles to something other than a dictionary is not a
cache file either.

<<imports>>=
import pickle
from typing import Any
<<cache load and save functions>>=
def read_cache_file(
    cache_file: Path,
) -> tuple[dict[Any, Any], Exception | None]:
    """Read the cache entries pickled in `cache_file`.

    Returns `(entries, None)` for a readable file, `({}, None)` when the
    file does not exist, and `({}, error)` when it exists but cannot be
    read as a cache.
    """
    try:
        with open(cache_file, "rb") as f:
            entries = pickle.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {}, None
    except Exception as error:
        return {}, error
    if not isinstance(entries, dict):
        return {}, TypeError(
            f"it holds a {type(entries).__name__}, not a dict"
        )
    return entries, None


def load_cache(provider_name: str) -> PersistentCache:
    """
    Load the cache for a provider from disk.

    Returns an empty cache if no cache file exists or if it cannot be
    read; an unreadable file is logged and is never overwritten by
    `save_cache`.
    """
    cache_file = get_cache_dir() / f"{provider_name}.pkl"
    entries, error = read_cache_file(cache_file)
    if error is not None:
        logger.warning(
            "Failed to load cache %s (%s); starting with an empty cache, "
            "and the file will not be overwritten",
            cache_file, error,
        )
    else:
        logger.debug(
            "Loaded cache for %s: %d entries", provider_name, len(entries)
        )
    return PersistentCache(entries)
@

\subsection{Saving a cache}

[[save_cache]] applies the rules of \cref{sec:cache-concurrency} in
order: skip an unchanged cache, then, holding the lock, re-read the file
and either merge with it or, if it cannot be read, keep it and write
beside it.
A plain dictionary has no flag, so it is always saved; that is what the
tests and other callers that build a cache by hand expect.
Only an I/O error or a value that cannot be pickled can stop a save;
either is logged, since the save usually runs in the exit hook, where
nobody could act on an exception.

<<imports>>=
from scholar.utils import atomic_write_bytes, file_lock
<<cache load and save functions>>=
def save_cache(provider_name: str, cache: dict[Any, Any]) -> None:
    """
    Save the cache for a provider to disk, adding to what is there.

    Under a lock, the file is re-read and replaced atomically by the
    union of its entries and `cache` (on a shared key, `cache` wins).
    A `PersistentCache` that was not changed is not saved.  A file that
    cannot be read is kept as it is, and `cache` goes to
    `<name>.pkl.unmerged-<pid>` beside it instead.
    """
    if isinstance(cache, PersistentCache) and not cache.changed:
        logger.debug(f"Cache for {provider_name} unchanged, not saved")
        return
    cache_file = get_cache_dir() / f"{provider_name}.pkl"
    try:
        with file_lock(cache_file, timeout=CACHE_LOCK_TIMEOUT):
            on_disk, error = read_cache_file(cache_file)
            if error is not None:
                <<keep the unreadable file, save [[cache]] beside it>>
                return
            <<replace the file with the union of [[on_disk]] and [[cache]]>>
    except (OSError, pickle.PicklingError) as e:
        logger.warning(f"Failed to save cache for {provider_name}: {e}")
@

The merge builds a new dictionary rather than updating either side: the
in-memory cache stays what the process put in it, and [[on_disk]] is
discarded anyway.
Only after the file is replaced is the cache marked unchanged, so a
failed write is retried by the next save, the exit hook's included.

<<replace the file with the union of [[on_disk]] and [[cache]]>>=
merged = {**on_disk, **cache}
atomic_write_bytes(cache_file, pickle.dumps(merged))
if isinstance(cache, PersistentCache):
    cache.changed = False
logger.debug(
    f"Saved cache for {provider_name}: {len(merged)} entries "
    f"({len(merged) - len(on_disk)} new)"
)
@

Each file beside an unreadable one gets a name of its own, made by
[[tempfile.mkstemp]], which creates the file exclusively and so never
picks a name that exists.
The name carries the UTC time it was written, so a listing sorts the
files by age.
A name made of the process id alone, as the first version did, was not
enough: process ids are reused, and a later process that met the same
unreadable file would have replaced an earlier one's entries.
The name does not end in [[.pkl]], so [[load_cache]] never takes it for
a cache; [[cache info]] lists these files and [[cache clear]] deletes
them (\cref{sec:cache-unmerged-files}).
Nothing merges one back automatically: the file it belongs to could not
be read, so there is nothing safe to merge it into.
The cache stays marked as changed, so a later save in the same process
tries the merge again.
An empty cache is not worth such a file (a process that ran
[[cache clear]] meets this only if the file reappeared unreadable).

<<imports>>=
import tempfile
from datetime import datetime, timezone
<<constants>>=
UNMERGED_SUFFIX = ".unmerged-"
<<keep the unreadable file, save [[cache]] beside it>>=
if cache:
    written = datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
    fd, name = tempfile.mkstemp(
        dir=cache_file.parent,
        prefix=f"{cache_file.name}{UNMERGED_SUFFIX}{written}-",
    )
    os.close(fd)
    unmerged = Path(name)
    atomic_write_bytes(unmerged, pickle.dumps(dict(cache)))
    logger.warning(
        "Cannot read cache %s (%s), so it is left as it is; "
        "this process's %d entries are in %s",
        cache_file, error, len(cache), unmerged,
    )
