\section{Test Configuration}
\label{sec:test-configuration}

The provider tests use caching, which can cause test pollution---cached results
from one test may be returned in another test, causing mock assertions to fail.
We provide a pytest fixture that isolates each test with its own cache directory.

The same fixture also unsets the contact-email variables
(\cref{sec:contact-email}).  Integrations such as Unpaywall only make
requests when an email is configured, so a developer who has
[[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]] set in their shell would otherwise turn offline unit tests
into live API calls.  Tests that need an email set it explicitly.

The provider API keys need the same treatment, for the same reason.
A provider constructed without an explicit key falls back to its
environment variable ([[ScopusProvider(api_key=None)]] reads
[[SCOPUS_API_KEY]]), so a test that means \enquote{no key} silently
becomes \enquote{the developer's key} on the developer's machine, and an
unmocked code path then spends a real quota---IEEE's is 200 requests a
day.
We therefore unset every variable a provider reads its credentials or
options from.
The list is explicit rather than a pattern such as [[*_API_KEY]] because
only scholar's own variables are ours to touch: the [[llm]] plugins'
keys belong to another tool, and the language-model tests substitute a
fake model anyway.
The network settings go too---scholar's proxy and User-Agent variables
and the standard proxy variables that [[requests]] honours---so that
every test sees the defaults (direct route, default User-Agent) whatever
the developer's shell exports.
The [[--proxy]] option lives in module state (\cref{ch:network}); a CLI
test that passes it would leave it set for every later test, so the
fixture clears that too.

The exception is the live integration tests (\cref{sec:live-tests}):
their whole point is to exercise the real API with the developer's real
credentials, and they read the keys from the environment to decide
whether to skip.
For them we keep the keys (and [[SCOPUS_VIEW]]); the contact email
stays unset for them as before, since no live test depends on it.

The fixture isolates the data directory ([[SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR]]) as well.
The data directory holds state that must survive [[scholar cache clear]],
such as the rate-limit bookkeeping of \cref{ch:ratelimit}---and that
persistence is exactly why isolation matters more here than for the cache:
a test that simulates an exhausted IEEE quota would otherwise record
\enquote{quota spent} in the developer's real data directory, making their
own [[scholar]] runs skip that provider for the rest of the day.

<<[[conftest.py]]>>=
