The provider objects themselves have the same problem
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/174}).
The registry builds one of each when [[scholar.providers]] is imported
(\cref{sec:provider-registry} in the providers chapter), during
collection, and each keyed provider reads its key from the environment
then and keeps it as an attribute.
So [[get_provider("s2")]] in a test held the developer's
[[S2_API_KEY]], and a registered provider's probe or search would have
sent it; only the network guard below kept it on the machine.
The fixture therefore sets each registered provider's key attributes
to [[None]] for every test, and monkeypatch restores them afterwards.
[[PROVIDER_KEY_ATTRIBUTES]], defined with the variables above, lists
the attributes the keyed constructors set: [[api_key]] (Semantic
Scholar, OpenAlex and its subclasses, IEEE, Scopus), Scopus's
[[inst_token]], and Web of Science's three keys.
Web of Science also derives its tier and endpoint from the key when it
is built, and a provider without a key must not claim a tier, so
[[_api_tier]] and [[_api_url]] are cleared with them.
The live tests keep the keys, as they keep the variables, since they
use the registered providers with the developer's real credentials.
Like [[pyalex]], the providers module is imported inside the fixture.

