The regression test runs a test in a separate pytest process whose
home directories ([[XDG_CACHE_HOME]], [[XDG_DATA_HOME]]) point into
[[tmp_path]] and already hold a cache, and checks that the run neither
changed nor added a file there.
The comparison covers the two home directories only, not the inner
run's own temporary directories, where its tests may leave files such as
the lock files of \cref{sec:cache-concurrency}.
A separate process is needed because the leak happens at import and at
exit, which a test inside the current process can observe neither of.
The inner test is one of the CLI's cache tests, but any test that
imports the CLI would do: the import loads the Crossref cache, and
before the fix the exit hook wrote it back to the fake home.
It wrote the same bytes it had read, so the comparison includes
modification times, and the test first dates the files back to 2001:
then a rewrite shows even on a file system with coarse timestamps.
The inner run also gets a temporary directory of its own, where pytest
keeps its [[tmp_path]] directories too, and no process directory may be
left there afterwards, so a run that fails to clean up fails the test.
[[platformdirs]] follows the XDG variables only on Linux, so elsewhere
the test is skipped.

