\subsection{What a request asks for}
\label{sec:request-targets}

Several modules put an identifier, mostly a DOI, into the path of a
URL, and a DOI may hold [[#]], [[?]], [[%]], a space or [[<>]]
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/304}).
Whether such a DOI reaches the service whole is decided below our code:
[[requests]] drops a fragment and re-quotes what it finds illegal, and
[[urllib3]] writes the request line.
A test that reads the URL our code built would miss both, so the
[[request_targets]] fixture lets those libraries run and looks at what
arrives.
It starts an HTTP server on the loopback interface that answers every
request with a 404 and an empty JSON object, and keeps the request
target of each, the path and query exactly as the request line
carried them.
A test names the hosts to stand in for ([[serve]]); inside
[[HTTPAdapter.send]], below every session, a request to one of them
goes to the server instead, as [[s2_loopback]] does for Semantic
Scholar alone (\cref{providers-module}).
A request to any other host still meets the network guard above.
The 404 is an answer every lookup understands as \enquote{no such
work}, so each test needs only the request.

The [[awkward_doi]] fixture is the invented DOI those tests send: one
of each of those characters in the suffix of an invented prefix.

<<[[conftest.py]]>>=


class RequestTargets:
    """A loopback stand-in for remote hosts that keeps request targets.

    `serve(*hosts)` routes requests for those hosts here; `targets`
    holds each request's target (path and query, as sent); `paths()`
    and `queries()` split and decode them.
    """

    def __init__(self):
        import http.server
        import threading

        self.hosts = set()
        self.targets = []
        recorder = self

        class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
            def do_GET(self):
                recorder.targets.append(self.path)
                body = b"{}"
                self.send_response(404)
                self.send_header("Content-Type", "application/json")
                self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
                self.end_headers()
                self.wfile.write(body)

            def log_message(self, *args):
                pass

        self.server = http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer(
            ("127.0.0.1", 0), Handler
        )
        self.base = f"http://127.0.0.1:{self.server.server_address[1]}"
        threading.Thread(
            target=self.server.serve_forever, daemon=True
        ).start()

    def serve(self, *hosts):
        """Stand in for `hosts` from now on."""
        self.hosts.update(hosts)

    def paths(self):
        """Return the percent-decoded path of each request."""
        from urllib.parse import unquote

        return [unquote(t.partition("?")[0]) for t in self.targets]

    def queries(self):
        """Return the decoded query parameters of each request."""
        from urllib.parse import parse_qs

        return [parse_qs(t.partition("?")[2]) for t in self.targets]

    def close(self):
        self.server.shutdown()
        self.server.server_close()


@pytest.fixture
def request_targets(monkeypatch):
    """Answer requests to the hosts a test names, keeping their targets."""
    from urllib.parse import urlsplit

    import requests.adapters

    recorder = RequestTargets()
    real_send = requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter.send

    def send(adapter, request, **kwargs):
        url = urlsplit(request.url)
        if url.hostname in recorder.hosts:
            request.url = recorder.base + request.url[
                len(f"{url.scheme}://{url.netloc}"):
            ]
        return real_send(adapter, request, **kwargs)

    monkeypatch.setattr(requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter, "send", send)
    yield recorder
    recorder.close()


