Environment variables are not the only state a test can leave behind.
The paper registry (\cref{sec:paper-registry}) is process-global, and
[[Paper.from_dict]] answers with the registered object whenever the
identifier is already known.
A test that loads a paper with some DOI therefore decides the title,
authors and abstract that every later test sees for that DOI, whatever
data the later test passes in.
Such a test passes alone and fails after the wrong neighbour, which
makes the failure look random.
Many tests already wrap themselves in [[isolated_registry]], but a test
cannot opt into isolation it does not know it needs.
So the fixture gives every test a fresh registry, using the same context
manager, which also restores the previous registry afterwards.

<<[[conftest.py]]>>=


@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)
def fresh_paper_registry():
    """Give each test an empty paper registry of its own."""
    from scholar.scholar import isolated_registry

    with isolated_registry() as registry:
        yield registry
