The rate limiters (\cref{ch:ratelimit}) are the same kind of state.
Their counts and cooldowns live in the data directory, which
[[isolate_cache]] already makes private to each test, but the limiter
objects themselves are shared through a module-level registry, and they
remember in memory what a later test must not inherit: the declared
limits (which Semantic Scholar re-declares when its key is rejected),
the time of the last request (which paces the next one), and whether
the process has already warned about a refusal (so a later test that
counts warnings would see none).
Tests used to evict the limiter they cared about by hand, before and
after, and a test that forgot to do so inherited whatever the previous
one had left.

Emptying the registry would be the obvious fix, but it would make each
test unlike a real process.
Importing [[scholar.providers]] registers a limiter for every provider
and importing [[scholar.crossref]], [[scholar.unpaywall]] and
[[scholar.europepmc]] registers one for each client (their module-level [[_client]] is built at import),
and [[scholar providers limits]] lists the clients only because of that
registration; a test of that command fails on an empty registry.
Worse, the providers built at import time would keep limiters the
registry no longer knows.
So the fixture puts the registry back to what it was before the test,
dropping the limiters the test created and resetting the remembered
state of the others.

``Back to what it was'' is only the import-time state if the imports
have happened by the time the snapshot is taken.
They have not: [[scholar.cli]] imports [[scholar.unpaywall]] lazily, so
the first test to run [[providers limits]] registered the Unpaywall
limiter in the middle of the test, the teardown restored a snapshot
without it, and the module stayed in [[sys.modules]], so nothing ever
registered it again (the outcome of the clients test then depended on
which tests ran before it).
The fixture therefore imports the registering modules first.
It does so inside the fixture body, not at the top of
[[conftest.py]]: the cache and data directories are redirected at
conftest import time, before any [[scholar]] module may load its
caches (see above).

<<[[conftest.py]]>>=


@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)
def fresh_rate_limiters():
    """Return the rate-limiter registry to its import-time state after
    each test: limiters created by the test are dropped, the others get
    back their declared limits and forget their last request and their
    warning."""
    # crossref, europepmc, providers and unpaywall register their
    # limiters when imported; see the prose above.
    from scholar import (  # noqa: F401
        crossref, europepmc, providers, ratelimit, unpaywall,
    )

    registered = dict(ratelimit._LIMITERS)
    declared = {name: lim.limits for name, lim in registered.items()}
    yield
    ratelimit._LIMITERS.clear()
    ratelimit._LIMITERS.update(registered)
    for name, limiter in registered.items():
        limiter.limits = declared[name]
        limiter._last_request = None
        limiter._warned = False
